          "guid": "ms60nq3iq2pz9hju",
          "message": {
            "text": "Detection is declared as DATA rather than a detect(projectRoot): boolean closure because the registry is transitively CLIENT code, and a closure would have pulled fs into the browser bundle.\n\nThe chain is not obvious: agentDisplayName.ts is the registry's first consumer, and it is re-exported into the client bundle (0). So registry.ts → every plugins/*.ts becomes client code the moment the client imports a display name.\n\nThe constraint turned out to improve the design rather than compromise it. All nine hand-written predicates — spread across aiInstructionsTools.ts and skills.ts, written independently over many tickets — are the same expression: any binary on PATH, or any of these paths present. So DetectionSpec loses no expressiveness, and detect.ts becomes the first entry in the docs/132 §132.9.1 host toolkit: one implementation, tested once, instead of nine predicates tested never.\n\nThe rule this establishes for later phases: types.ts, registry.ts and plugins/** import no node builtins. A capability needing the filesystem takes it as an injected dependency (see DetectDeps) or lives in a server-only sibling. Phase 4 moves the drive capability, which spawns processes — that one has to land in a sibling, not on the plugin object.\n\nVerified: npm run build:client succeeds and the bundle contains no fs import after this change.\n",
            "markdown": "Detection is declared as DATA rather than a `detect(projectRoot): boolean` closure because the registry is transitively CLIENT code, and a closure would have pulled `fs` into the browser bundle.\n\nThe chain is not obvious: `agentDisplayName.ts` is the registry's first consumer, and it is [re-exported into the client bundle](0). So `registry.ts` → every `plugins/*.ts` becomes client code the moment the client imports a display name.\n\nThe constraint turned out to improve the design rather than compromise it. All nine hand-written predicates — spread across `aiInstructionsTools.ts` and `skills.ts`, written independently over many tickets — are the same expression: *any binary on PATH, or any of these paths present*. So `DetectionSpec` loses no expressiveness, and `detect.ts` becomes the first entry in the docs/132 §132.9.1 host toolkit: one implementation, tested once, instead of nine predicates tested never.\n\n**The rule this establishes for later phases:** `types.ts`, `registry.ts` and `plugins/**` import no node builtins. A capability needing the filesystem takes it as an injected dependency (see `DetectDeps`) or lives in a server-only sibling. Phase 4 moves the drive capability, which spawns processes — that one has to land in a sibling, not on the plugin object.\n\nVerified: `npm run build:client` succeeds and the bundle contains no `fs` import after this change.\n"
          },
          "locations": [
